{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"9c4ca62c-a174-475b-9041-f09bfc5a158a","title":"Radians: arc length on a circle"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"9c4ca62c-a174-475b-9041-f09bfc5a158a","step":{"id":"s1","script":"An angle in radians compares the arc it sweeps out with the circle’s radius. Start with a radius of three centimeters.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Radians compare arc and radius"},{"op":"figure","id":"circlefig","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-3.7,3.7],"y":[-3.7,3.7],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"circle","id":"rim","center":[0,0],"r":3},{"kind":"point","id":"center","at":[0,0],"label":"O"},{"kind":"point","id":"start","at":[3,0]},{"kind":"segment","id":"radius","from":{"at":"center"},"to":{"at":"start"},"label":"r = 3 cm"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"9c4ca62c-a174-475b-9041-f09bfc5a158a","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Sweep one radian. The curved arc is exactly as long as the radius: three centimeters. Measure the arc, not the straight chord.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"onerad","on":"circlefig","marks":[{"kind":"arc","id":"unitarc","center":{"at":"center"},"r":3,"start":0,"end":57.2957795,"label":"s = 3 cm"},{"kind":"point","id":"end","at":{"on":"unitarc","t":1}},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"center"},"to":{"at":"end"}},{"kind":"angle","at":{"at":"center"},"from":{"at":"start"},"to":{"at":"end"},"r":0.22,"label":"1 rad"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"definition","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"\\theta=\\frac{s}{r}\\quad\\text{(radians)}"},{"op":"equation","id":"onecalc","anchor":{"el":"definition","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\theta=\\frac{3\\,\\text{cm}}{3\\,\\text{cm}}=1\\,\\text{rad}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"9c4ca62c-a174-475b-9041-f09bfc5a158a","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Double the arc length on this same circle, and the angle doubles. Rearranging the definition gives arc length equals radius times angle in radians.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"tworad","on":"circlefig","marks":[{"kind":"arc","id":"secondarc","center":{"at":"center"},"r":3,"start":57.2957795,"end":114.591559,"label":"+3 cm"},{"kind":"point","id":"endtwo","at":{"on":"secondarc","t":1}},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"center"},"to":{"at":"endtwo"},"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"text","at":[-1.5,1.5],"text":"2 rad"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"arclaw","anchor":{"el":"onecalc","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"s=r\\theta=3(2)=6\\,\\text{cm}"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"On a circle of radius 3 cm, what arc length does an angle of 0.5 radians sweep out?","expected_gist":"1.5 cm, using s = rθ"}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"9c4ca62c-a174-475b-9041-f09bfc5a158a","step":{"id":"s4","script":"A full turn sweeps the entire circumference. Dividing by the radius shows why one full turn is two pi radians.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"fullturn","on":"circlefig","marks":[{"kind":"arc","center":{"at":"center"},"r":3.4,"start":0,"end":355,"arrow":true},{"kind":"text","at":[0,-2],"text":"full turn"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"turncalc","anchor":{"el":"arclaw","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"\\theta_{\\text{full}}=\\frac{2\\pi r}{r}=2\\pi\\,\\text{rad}=360^\\circ"}],"checkpoint":null}}
